<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peng Dehuai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a>–<a href="/years/1974">1974</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peng Dehuai was a Chinese Communist general who commanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-volunteer-army">Chinese People's Volunteers</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> from 1950 and later served as minister of defence. After he criticised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lu">Lushan</a> Conference of 1959 he was dismissed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>; persecuted during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a>, he died in prison in 1974 and was rehabilitated in 1978.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peng-dehuai.jpg" alt="Peng Dehuai" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ​《新华社》记者 郑景康 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_Peng_Dehuai_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Peng Dehuai was born on 24 October 1898 in the village of Shixiang, Xiangtan County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a>, into a poor peasant family. His grand-uncle had fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion</a> and told him of its ideals, that everyone should have enough food, women should not bind their feet and land should be redistributed equally. Poverty forced him to stop his education at ten, and he worked as a manual labourer. In March 1916 he joined a Hunan warlord army as a private, and over ten years rose to major in the armies of several warlords. One of his commanding officers, a Nationalist veteran of the 1911 revolution, led him to sympathise with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a>'s aims of social reform and national reunification.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1926 his forces joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a>, and he took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-expedition">Northern Expedition</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuhan">Wuhan</a> government's defeat he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>, allying himself with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> and Zhu De. He helped defend the Jiangxi Soviet and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-march">Long March</a>: of the 18,000 men under his command at its start in October 1934, only about 3,000 reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> in October 1935. In the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> he argued that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> should genuinely fight the Japanese, against Mao's wish for token resistance. After 1945 he commanded the Northwest Field Army, and defeated the Kuomintang in Northwest China.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> on 25 June 1950, and Peng was among the few senior leaders who backed Mao's proposal to intervene. He directly commanded the Chinese People's Volunteer Army for the first half of the war. Convinced that the army had to be more professional and better equipped, he became Minister of National Defense in 1954 and began to reform it on the Soviet model. In January 1955 he attacked and occupied a chain of islands held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a>. He resisted Mao's personality cult during the 1950s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a>, begun in 1958, caused a man-made famine in which tens of millions starved by 1959. At the Lushan Conference of July 1959 Mao invited criticism and promised not to attack anyone personally, and Peng, critical of Mao's leadership, spoke. Mao won the confrontation, labelled Peng the leader of an anti-Party clique and in September replaced him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lin-biao">Lin Biao</a>, who reversed his reforms.</p><p>Peng lived under constant surveillance near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. In 1966 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiang-qing">Jiang Qing</a> directed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-guards-china">Red Guards</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> to arrest him, and they abducted him in chains on 25 December 1966. He died on 29 November 1974. The party leadership concealed his death for years, and his wife, Pu Anxiu, learned of it only on returning to Beijing in 1978, when the news was first publicly disclosed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peng Dehuai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In October 1950 Chinese People’s Volunteers under <b>Peng Dehuai</b> entered the Korean War, drove United Nations forces back from the Yalu River and fought them to a stalemate before the armistice of July 1953, at a cost of hundreds of thousands of Chinese dead.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In China the Great Leap Forward led to a famine in 1959–1961 that killed tens of millions; when the defence minister <b>Peng Dehuai</b> criticised the policy at the Lushan Conference in 1959 he was purged, and officials went on inflating harvest figures.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>The East Is Red began as a peasant love song of the Loess Plateau; its modern words of 1942 idealise Mao Zedong, and when a political commissar proposed teaching it to the army in 1956 the defence minister <b>Peng Dehuai</b> refused and called it a personality cult.</blockquote><a href="/essays/how-songs-travel">How Songs Travel</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1956 a political commissar proposed teaching it to the army; the defence minister, <b>Peng Dehuai</b>, refused and called it a personality cult.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-east-is-red">The East Is Red</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Xiangtan County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=27.78,112.95&amp;year=1947" aria-label="Xiangtan County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7513 1146 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Peng Dehuai"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7963 1416h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7963 1416h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=27.78,112.95&amp;year=1947">What was here in 1947? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
